        "description": "<div id=\"link_wrapped_content\">\n<body><section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>Budget PC gamers have had little to celebrate in 2026. Intel may be trying to change that.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>It\u2019s hard not to feel despondent about the state of PC building right now. Data centers are sucking up memory, prices for parts are climbing higher than ever, and all the major players seem more interested in AI revenue than anything else. Gamers would be forgiven for feeling abandoned, as companies <a href=\"https://www.pcworld.com/article/3013044/the-post-geforce-era-what-if-nvidia-abandons-pc-gaming.html\">move to class PC parts as part of their \u201cedge AI\u201d strategy</a> rather than a core component of their business.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>Yet while Nvidia is trying to get everyone to throw their annual budgets at AI and AMD is <a href=\"https://www.pcworld.com/article/3139796/amd-is-bringing-fsr-4-to-older-radeon-cards-but-youll-have-to-wait.html\">slow-rolling its upscale",
